{Opponent info: Runner up GBR Ch 1963; Grade 2a ECF = 225-232} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 {Franklin played the Stonewall attack at Hastings and I didn't want to play against this.} 3. Bf4 Bg7 4. Nbd2 O-O 5. e3 d6 6. h3 Nbd7 7. Bc4 {Probably played to provoke 7......d5 so he can get his Knight on e5 and play a Stonewall attack.} a6 8. a4 Ne8 9. c3 e5 10. Bg5 Nef6 11. O-O c6 {Threatens 12.....d5 but creates a weakness at d6.} 12. dxe5 dxe5 (12... Nxe5 13. Nxe5 dxe5 14. Ne4 {and I have tokeep the Queen defending the Knight on f6.}) 13. Ne4 h6 (13... Qc7 14. Qd6 {is good for White.}) 14. Bh4 g5 {Creates KS weaknesses but seems the best.} 15. Nxf6+ {The sacrifice on g5 doesn't work.} Bxf6 16. Bg3 Qe7 17. Nh2 Bg7 18. Ba2 {White wants to play f4 but must open the a6-g1 diagonal with his Bishop on c4.} Nc5 19. f4 Ne4 {A useful intermediate move.} 20. Be1 Be6 21. f5 {This surprised me. It seems to relieve the tension.} Bxa2 22. Rxa2 Nf6 23. Ng4 Rad8 {Gaining control of the d-file.} 24. Qe2 Rd6 25. e4 {I had ideas of playing ......e4.} Rfd8 26. h4 Nxg4 27. Qxg4 Rd1 {Hampering White's attacking plans.} 28. b4 {or else I can play ......c5, ......c4, ?d3. This brings his aRook into play.} f6 {so as to be able to move the Queen.} 29. hxg5 hxg5 {sounder than fx?} (29... fxg5) 30. Qe2 (30. Qh5 Qf7 {would force the Queens off.}) 30... Qd7 {Black forces them off now anyway.} 31. Qc4+ Qf7 32. Qxf7+ Kxf7 33. Kf2 Bf8 (33... Bh6 34. Rh1 Kg7 35. g4) 34. Ke2 Rb1 ({Not} 34... c5 35. b5) ({or} 34... a5 35. b5) 35. Bf2 Rxf1 36. Kxf1 Rd3 37. Rc2 {Adjudicated Drawn The match was a tie 4-4} 1/2-1/2

