1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. Bf4 Bg7 4. e3 O-O 5. Nbd2 d6 6. Bc4 ({The opening is a favourite of Franklin's. It is here slightly more precise to play} 6. c3 {or 6.h3 (to safeguard the bishop from exchange by Nh5), leaving the option of developing the king bishop at c4, d3 or e2 according to the formation Black chooses (Barden)}) 6... Nfd7 ({This and the next move are stronger than the older, routine idea of} 6... Nbd7 {and ...c5 (Barden)}) 7. c3 Nc6 8. e4 ({"It is better to} 8. O-O {and, if} e5 {,} 9. Bg3 {" (Lee, CHESS)}) (8. h4 $5 e5 9. Bg5 Qe8 10. h5 exd4 11. hxg6 hxg6 12. cxd4 Nb6 13. Bb3 Be6 14. Bc2 a5 {Franklin-Saunders, corr 1977 (0-1, 53 moves)}) 8... e5 9. dxe5 $2 {"Releasing the tension gives Black no problems and renders 7 c3 pointless." (Lee)} Ncxe5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. Bxe5 $2 (11. Be2 {Barden}) 11... Bxe5 12. O-O c6 13. f4 Bg7 14. Nf3 Qe7 15. Qd3 d5 16. exd5 Qc5+ 17. Rf2 Bf5 18. Qf1 cxd5 19. Bd3 Be4 20. Ng5 Rfe8 21. Bb5 Re7 22. Kh1 a6 23. Nxe4 Qxb5 24. Qxb5 axb5 25. Ng3 f5 26. a3 d4 27. cxd4 Bxd4 28. Rc2 Rxa3 29. bxa3 Bxa1 30. Kg1 Bd4+ 31. Kf1 b6 32. Rc6 Bc5 33. Ne2 Re3 34. Ng1 Rxa3 35. Nf3 b4 36. Ne5 b3 37. Rc8+ Kg7 38. Rc7+ Kf6 {Sources: BCM, July 1966, p199; CHESS, September 1966, Vol.31/512-13, p396 (wrongly ascribed to the Southend Easter Congress); The Guardian, 22 July 1966} 0-1

