1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. e3 e6 4. Bd3 c5 5. c3 Nc6 6. Nbd2 Bd6 7. O-O O-O 8. dxc5 Bxc5 9. e4 Qc7 10. Qe2 Bd6 11. Re1 Ng4 12. Nf1 Nce5 13. Nxe5 Nxe5 14. Bc2 dxe4 15. Bxe4 f5 16. Bc2 Ng6 17. Bb3 Kh8 18. Bg5 e5 19. Rad1 e4 20. g3 $5 Ne5 21. Bf4 $1 Re8 $5 (21... Nf3+ $4 22. Qxf3 exf3 23. Bxd6 Qd8 24. Bb4 $18 {Wilson in BPCF Information Circular Feb 1996 p32. His notes don't mention most of the turning points in this comedy of errors.}) 22. Bxe5 Bxe5 23. Qb5 Re7 24. Ne3 Bd7 25. Qb4 $14 Ree8 26. Nd5 (26. Ba4 $142) (26. Qc4 $142) 26... Qc6 27. Ne7 Qc7 28. f4 a5 (28... exf3 $4 29. Ng6+ hxg6 30. Qh4# {Wilson}) 29. Qa3 a4 (29... Be6 $1 $11 {15.01/0 Stockfish}) 30. Bf7 Rf8 31. Bh5 Bf6 32. Nd5 Qc6 33. Ne3 $2 {Now the initiative passes to Black.} (33. Be2) 33... g6 34. Be2 Rad8 35. Qb4 Be6 36. Bc4 Bxc4 37. Nxc4 Qa6 $2 {A mistake not indicated by Wilson but he spotted the flaw} (37... b5 $17 {among other moves}) 38. Rd7 $1 b5 39. Na3 (39. Red1 $1 {was the correct follow-up}) 39... Qe6 40. Red1 Be7 $1 $11 41. Qd4+ $4 {This should lose} (41. Qxb5 $8) 41... Bf6 $4 {"Normal service" resumes after this - but not for long} (41... Kg8 $1 $19 42. Nxb5 $140 Bc5 $1 {Black wins the exchange; lines like this show how computers completely changed correspondence chess.}) 42. Qc5 Be7 43. Qxb5 Rxd7 44. Rxd7 Bxa3 45. bxa3 Rf7 46. Rxf7 Qxf7 47. c4 Qf6 {Wilson: "White seems to be winning now, so what goes wrong? TH: Stockfish shows White has no objective advantage, though of course it is harder to play Black.} 48. Qe5 Kg7 49. c5 {Therefore the ?? that Wilson attached to this move was misplaced . His comment here ("The game is a draw now but White played on") and the sequel seems to show that neither player understood what was going on here. Black now makes a suicidal move but eventually gets away with it.} Qxe5 $4 {This should lose because it creates a self-protecting pair of passed pawns for the opponent. It is surprising that the OTB master Franklin totally misplays the resulting king and pawn endgame. The final crosstable of this section was in BPCF IC Nov 1994 p9, showing Franklin scored 3.5/8, finishing sixth in this semifinal. He was probably giving more attention to S49.} (49... Kf7 $8 50. Qd5+ $140 {Several moves draw} Qe6 (50... Ke7)) 50. fxe5 Kf7 51. c6 Ke6 52. Kf2 Ke7 53. h4 $5 {Unnecessary but White is still better after it.} (53. Ke3 $142 Ke6 54. Kd4 {Threatening to reach a winning Q endgame by K-c5-b6 etc so BK must retreat} Ke7 (54... h6 55. Kc5 e3 56. Kb6 e2 57. c7) 55. h3 g5 (55... Ke6 56. Kc5 {etc}) (55... Kd8 56. e6 {Now the Black K cannot move} g5 57. Kd5 e3 58. Kd6 {Black may promote but White will mate.})) 53... Ke6 54. Ke3 $6 {Imprecise} (54. Ke2 $142 {to meet} h6 {by} 55. Ke3) 54... h5 $2 (54... h6 $142 {should draw according to the engine}) 55. Kf4 {Not a mistake in itself but a step in the wrong direction; perhaps Franklin was analysing the wrong position?} Ke7 56. Kg5 $4 {TH: Wilson didn't remark that this is a huge blunder, changing a won ending to a loss.} e3 0-1

